WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.452
Welcome to Expedition News Network. Today we are looking back at the Endurance

00:00:07.452 --> 00:00:10.318
expedition, from 1914 to 1916.

00:00:10.318 --> 00:00:15.314
Our question is: how did a plan to cross Antarctica become a mission

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:16.857
to bring people home?

00:00:16.857 --> 00:00:22.934
Two ideas help explain the story: changing conditions required new plans, and people

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:26.045
with different skills had to work together.

00:00:26.045 --> 00:00:30.628
First, our research desk explains the original goal.

00:00:30.628 --> 00:00:37.389
Shackleton planned to cross Antarctica. Endurance carried his party toward the Weddell Sea,

00:00:37.389 --> 00:00:40.361
but the ship became trapped in pack ice.

00:00:40.361 --> 00:00:45.456
Pack ice is floating sea ice crowded together. The ship drifted with it

00:00:45.456 --> 00:00:48.255
instead of following the planned route.

00:00:48.255 --> 00:00:53.928
When pressure damaged the ship in 1915, the party had to leave it.

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:58.749
That connects a condition to a decision: the damaged ship could no longer

00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:01.721
provide a safe base for the original mission.

00:01:01.721 --> 00:01:07.533
Here is a short quotation from Shackleton's book South: Stores, dogs, sledges, and

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:12.352
equipment were ready to be moved from the ship at a moment's notice.

00:01:12.352 --> 00:01:17.229
This detail supports the idea that the group prepared for a change before

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:18.030
it happened.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:24.360
However, this is the leader's account, written after the journey. It helps explain

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.378
his preparations, but does not tell us what every person thought.

00:01:29.378 --> 00:01:34.376
Now look at the route diagram. It begins at the Weddell Sea ice

00:01:34.376 --> 00:01:39.056
camp, then moves to Elephant Island, then to South Georgia.

00:01:39.056 --> 00:01:45.370
In April 1916, the party reached Elephant Island in three small boats.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:49.947
Six men then took the James Caird toward South Georgia to seek help

00:01:49.947 --> 00:01:51.518
for those who remained.

00:01:51.518 --> 00:01:57.247
The arrows show the order of destinations. They are not exact sailing tracks

00:01:57.247 --> 00:01:59.132
or a measure of distance.

00:01:59.132 --> 00:02:05.653
The James Caird needed preparation. Carpenter Harry McNish worked on the boat, supplies

00:02:05.653 --> 00:02:11.499
had to fit into limited space, and Frank Worsley's navigation helped guide the

00:02:11.499 --> 00:02:11.874
crew.

00:02:11.874 --> 00:02:17.003
These were different jobs connected to the same purpose: reaching help.

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:22.475
Preparation could reduce some risks, but it could not make the voyage certain

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:23.256
to succeed.

00:02:23.256 --> 00:02:27.776
This is why our report explains people's work as well as naming the

00:02:27.776 --> 00:02:29.125
places they reached.

00:02:29.125 --> 00:02:34.652
We compared Shackleton's memoir with a museum chronology to check the order of

00:02:34.652 --> 00:02:35.148
events.

00:02:35.148 --> 00:02:41.484
A Frank Hurley photograph, cataloged by Royal Museums Greenwich, is another kind of

00:02:41.484 --> 00:02:43.927
evidence about the ship and ice.

00:02:43.927 --> 00:02:49.904
A photograph records a visible moment. It cannot prove every cause or tell

00:02:49.904 --> 00:02:51.600
us what someone felt.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.764
Our newsroom background is an illustration, so we do not use it as

00:02:56.764 --> 00:02:58.328
historical evidence.

00:02:58.328 --> 00:03:04.682
The remaining Endurance party was rescued from Elephant Island in August 1916. All

00:03:04.682 --> 00:03:08.091
twenty-eight members of that party survived.

00:03:08.091 --> 00:03:14.326
The wider expedition also included a separate Ross Sea party, which suffered losses,

00:03:14.326 --> 00:03:17.369
so we must be precise about whom we mean.

00:03:17.369 --> 00:03:23.318
The planned crossing was not completed. The rescue was a different achievement.

00:03:23.318 --> 00:03:29.428
Our evidence shows how changing plans and working together shaped the outcome. Thank

00:03:29.428 --> 00:03:32.411
you for watching Expedition News Network.
